WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.540
All the things we know of the properties of the cancer cell could be traced to 

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:06.990
the malfunction of genes.

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:07.730
That,

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:08.460
in turn,

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:11.150
has raised the possibility of very,

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.730
very precise therapeutics,

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:21.500
because if you can identify the malfunctioning genes in any individual 

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:22.150
tumor,

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:23.910
you can,

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:24.750
in principle,

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:30.950
devise a therapeutic that 

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:34.250
specifically targets that malfunction.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.350
And we call this targeted therapy. 

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:45.150
Actually the very first example was in breast cancer many years ago, 

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:49.350
when it was discovered that the estrogen receptor, 

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:57.750
the molecule that the hormone estrogen binds to,

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:01.950
and that leads to the effect of estrogen on breast tissue, 

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:08.570
that if you inhibited that, you would have a favorable 

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:11.420
effect on many breast cancers.

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:16.150
And it turned out that these breast cancers were expressing the estrogen receptor.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:21.610
Those that didn't express the estrogen receptor did not respond to 

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:22.510
the therapeutic.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:27.660
So that's a thirty year old version of 

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:32.600
targeted therapy. Flash forward to our 

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:37.180
current knowledge about the malfunctioning genes of cancer cells,

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:41.910
the next triumph was with a relatively rare disease, known as 

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:43.970
chronic myeloid leukemia.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.100
This was an inevitably fatal disease.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:52.490
The standard chemotherapeutics had marginal 

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.600
utility with the disease,

00:01:55.790 --> 00:02:00.600
and we knew that there was a particular malfunctioning 

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:05.013
gene in those leukemia cells. And 

00:02:06.153 --> 00:02:07.793
working with a drug company,

00:02:08.483 --> 00:02:11.223
a young medical scientist named Brian Druker 

00:02:12.933 --> 00:02:17.723
demonstrated that an existing therapeutic that was on the shelf, not being 

00:02:17.723 --> 00:02:21.893
used, was effective against that 

00:02:23.783 --> 00:02:28.653
malfunctioning gene that was common to all chronic 

00:02:28.663 --> 00:02:33.293
myeloid leukemia. And the very first tests 

00:02:33.983 --> 00:02:37.593
of that therapeutic with patients with chronic myeloid leukemia,

00:02:38.043 --> 00:02:40.193
had what I would call a Lazarus effect.

00:02:40.953 --> 00:02:41.293
I mean,

00:02:41.303 --> 00:02:46.243
patients were on their deathbed and literally stood up within days of getting 

00:02:46.243 --> 00:02:48.893
this therapeutic and walked out of the hospital.

00:02:49.773 --> 00:02:50.893
It was astonishing.

00:02:52.923 --> 00:02:54.693
And we have other examples now.

00:02:58.253 --> 00:03:02.493
What we need is a complete catalogue of 

00:03:02.933 --> 00:03:07.613
those genetic malfunctions in various kinds of cancer that are common 

00:03:07.613 --> 00:03:11.493
enough to create therapeutics that can then be ready to go 

00:03:12.383 --> 00:03:14.093
for any individual patient.

00:03:15.283 --> 00:03:20.243
We need the ability for every hospital to test every 

00:03:20.243 --> 00:03:24.923
individual tumor for all the genetic malformations, malfunctions, 

00:03:25.033 --> 00:03:26.943
for which we have therapeutics,

00:03:28.593 --> 00:03:31.393
and we're rapidly approaching that capability.

00:03:34.883 --> 00:03:39.293
The information in our genes is encoded by an 

00:03:39.293 --> 00:03:43.603
alphabet of four molecules called 

00:03:43.603 --> 00:03:44.613
nucleotides,

00:03:45.073 --> 00:03:48.373
and it's a simple alphabet,

00:03:51.163 --> 00:03:55.583
the order in which the letters are arranged encode 

00:03:55.693 --> 00:04:00.533
all the functions of a cell. The first time that that order 

00:04:00.533 --> 00:04:01.343
was determined,

00:04:01.979 --> 00:04:06.809
it cost a huge amount of money and took a long time to do-- 

00:04:06.819 --> 00:04:07.519
years.

00:04:07.729 --> 00:04:12.649
Now it can be done in a day, for less than a thousand dollars.

00:04:13.449 --> 00:04:16.609
We call this complete genome sequencing.

00:04:16.609 --> 00:04:21.559
The genome is a term that simply refers to the entire genetic 

00:04:21.699 --> 00:04:25.299
information contained in a cell.

00:04:25.859 --> 00:04:26.709
Right now,

00:04:26.719 --> 00:04:31.509
most advanced cancer 

00:04:31.509 --> 00:04:34.639
centers will either 

00:04:36.199 --> 00:04:40.199
determine the complete sequence of that alphabet 

00:04:42.689 --> 00:04:46.229
in the genome of a patient's cancer cell.

00:04:47.459 --> 00:04:51.399
You compare that to the genome from their normal cells,

00:04:51.609 --> 00:04:56.549
and you can spot any genetic malformation that gives rise 

00:04:56.549 --> 00:04:57.499
to a malfunction.

00:04:59.189 --> 00:05:03.319
So you get a read out of potential targets 

00:05:03.889 --> 00:05:05.099
for therapeutics.

00:05:06.089 --> 00:05:08.519
This is a work in progress.

00:05:09.889 --> 00:05:14.809
And the biggest impediment to a completion of 

00:05:14.809 --> 00:05:16.669
the project is data analysis.

00:05:17.149 --> 00:05:22.129
The data are accumulating more rapidly than the bioinformaticists can decipher it.

00:05:26.189 --> 00:05:27.299
What's the catch here?

00:05:28.609 --> 00:05:32.899
The catch here is a very old and daunting 

00:05:34.689 --> 00:05:35.199
enemy,

00:05:36.189 --> 00:05:37.839
and that's called drug resistance.

00:05:39.169 --> 00:05:40.099
Sooner or later,

00:05:41.039 --> 00:05:43.399
even with an exquisitely targeted therapy,

00:05:45.339 --> 00:05:48.099
such as the one I described for chronic myeloid leukemia,

00:05:48.099 --> 00:05:49.999
the original drug is called Gleevec,

00:05:50.389 --> 00:05:52.519
and it was truly a miracle drug,

00:05:53.389 --> 00:05:54.219
sooner or later,

00:05:54.399 --> 00:05:56.189
the tumors develop resistance.

00:05:56.789 --> 00:05:59.779
The first therapeutic works for a while,

00:05:59.789 --> 00:06:03.159
and then you have to have another one, that works for a while,

00:06:03.169 --> 00:06:04.319
and you have to have the another one.

00:06:04.749 --> 00:06:04.889
Now,

00:06:04.889 --> 00:06:06.019
why is this happening?

00:06:06.709 --> 00:06:11.239
The reason this is happening is something that we did not appreciate until very 

00:06:11.239 --> 00:06:11.819
recently.

00:06:12.729 --> 00:06:13.369
And that is,

00:06:13.379 --> 00:06:18.149
if you take a large tumor that's got trillions of 

00:06:18.149 --> 00:06:18.819
cells in it,

00:06:19.709 --> 00:06:21.839
and you look at the individual cells,

00:06:21.839 --> 00:06:25.219
which we can now do, if you look at them one by one 

00:06:26.309 --> 00:06:31.219
for the order of the alphabet in their genome,

00:06:32.309 --> 00:06:37.199
I think it's safe to say that no two cells in a tumor are 

00:06:37.199 --> 00:06:38.359
exactly alike,

00:06:38.599 --> 00:06:39.229
genetically.

00:06:39.239 --> 00:06:40.979
The differences may be very small,

00:06:41.339 --> 00:06:42.919
but they're not exactly alike.

00:06:43.509 --> 00:06:48.429
That means that there are a trillion possibilities for 

00:06:49.109 --> 00:06:54.059
a particular form of drug resistance to be lurking in 

00:06:54.059 --> 00:06:55.099
the mass of a tumor.

00:06:55.099 --> 00:06:59.819
So you kill off 99.9% of the cells and what's left? 

00:07:00.539 --> 00:07:05.169
That rare cell in which the resistance to the drug has 

00:07:05.169 --> 00:07:08.879
already developed, simply by genetic accident.

00:07:09.099 --> 00:07:10.019
It's simple,

00:07:10.619 --> 00:07:15.349
just random genetic variation, which creates this 

00:07:15.349 --> 00:07:18.229
incredible, what we call heterogeneity, tumor heterogeneity.

00:07:19.409 --> 00:07:23.519
This is only recently appreciated and it is a daunting challenge.

00:07:24.609 --> 00:07:27.579
And we don't quite know how to deal with it yet.

00:07:27.589 --> 00:07:29.499
How do we deal with tumor heterogeneity?

00:07:30.529 --> 00:07:31.529
Because until then,

00:07:31.529 --> 00:07:34.419
we are going to have this relentless treadmill.

00:07:35.669 --> 00:07:37.819
And one approach,

00:07:37.819 --> 00:07:38.459
of course,

00:07:38.469 --> 00:07:40.499
is a time honored mechanism,

00:07:40.499 --> 00:07:42.119
what we call combination therapy.

00:07:42.959 --> 00:07:47.219
So some tumors have been cured with conventional chemotherapy 

00:07:47.809 --> 00:07:50.819
by combining several drugs at one time.

00:07:54.009 --> 00:07:55.629
We think we know how that works,

00:07:55.639 --> 00:07:58.409
but we also know it doesn't always work,

00:07:59.309 --> 00:08:00.419
and sooner or later,

00:08:01.309 --> 00:08:05.867
the patients who have responded even to combination therapy, die of their 

00:08:05.867 --> 00:08:06.487
disease.

00:08:06.967 --> 00:08:07.587
To me,

00:08:07.587 --> 00:08:09.867
that's the great present challenge.

00:08:10.917 --> 00:08:14.207
How do we deal with this extraordinary heterogeneity? 

00:08:18.807 --> 00:08:23.027
That brings onto the scene immunotherapy.

00:08:24.337 --> 00:08:27.467
Immunotherapy is different because it's not 

00:08:29.417 --> 00:08:34.337
a chemical like chemotherapy or like targeted 

00:08:34.337 --> 00:08:38.867
therapy that we talked about. Immunotherapy utilizes either immune 

00:08:38.867 --> 00:08:42.127
cells or the antibodies made by immune cells 

00:08:43.717 --> 00:08:48.457
to either unleash the immune 

00:08:48.457 --> 00:08:50.447
system to kill tumor cells, 

00:08:55.217 --> 00:08:59.547
well by one means or another, unleashes the immune system to kill tumor cells.

00:09:00.417 --> 00:09:00.947
Well,

00:09:01.417 --> 00:09:04.027
there's nothing new under the sun after a fashion.

00:09:06.517 --> 00:09:09.727
This works in some people and not in others,

00:09:10.587 --> 00:09:11.827
and it works for a while,

00:09:12.817 --> 00:09:14.877
and with a few exceptions,

00:09:15.017 --> 00:09:18.847
ultimately it fails because of resistance.

00:09:19.917 --> 00:09:23.627
And the resistance is distinctive from the kind that 

00:09:26.387 --> 00:09:30.857
occurs for targeted pharmacological 

00:09:30.857 --> 00:09:31.427
therapy,

00:09:32.817 --> 00:09:37.667
but it is again of a sort that 

00:09:37.677 --> 00:09:42.457
can arise spontaneously in that lonely 

00:09:42.457 --> 00:09:45.847
cell amongst the trillion of a tumor.

00:09:47.777 --> 00:09:49.627
It appears that some people,

00:09:50.087 --> 00:09:50.957
some tumors,

00:09:51.037 --> 00:09:54.937
have this in advance of receiving immunotherapy.

00:09:55.227 --> 00:09:55.727
Others,

00:09:56.317 --> 00:09:57.707
it appears later,

00:09:57.707 --> 00:10:00.627
after you've killed off much of the tumors.

00:10:01.617 --> 00:10:02.027
Now,

00:10:02.677 --> 00:10:07.608
can we combine immunotherapy with the pharmacological targeted therapy?

00:10:07.608 --> 00:10:08.308
Absolutely,

00:10:08.798 --> 00:10:12.108
it's just, this is all very recent.

00:10:12.798 --> 00:10:17.578
So this is just now underway and how effective it will be 

00:10:17.578 --> 00:10:18.528
remains to be seen.

00:10:19.798 --> 00:10:22.258
You're coming at the tumor in two very different ways,

00:10:22.258 --> 00:10:22.908
and that's good.

00:10:24.798 --> 00:10:29.208
How efficacious it will be remains to be seen, it's too early to know. 

